{"chunk_id": "c838eacc390b", "text": "At a grand festival on Mount Raivataka, the Vrishnis and Andhakas gather in splendor with music, gifts, drinking, and noble families present. Krishna and Arjuna wander through the festivities and notice Krishna\u2019s beautiful sister, Subhadra (here called Bhadra). Arjuna is immediately struck by desire. Teasing him, Krishna suggests that instead of relying on a risky self-choice marriage ceremony, Arjuna should abduct her in the warrior tradition. With Yudhishthira\u2019s approval, Arjuna prepares a chariot and waits. When Subhadra finishes worship at the mountain shrine and departs, Arjuna seizes the moment\u2014literally carrying her away in his chariot toward Indraprastha. Her guards rush to Dwaraka and raise the alarm, and the Yadava warriors, inflamed with wine and pride, begin arming themselves in outrage at Arjuna\u2019s bold act.\nDuring a grand festival, Krishna encourages Arjuna to abduct his sister Subhadra for marriage, and Arjuna seizes her, provoking outrage among the Yadava warriors.\nThemes: festival gathering, romantic desire, marriage by abduction, kinship politics, warrior honor", "vector": [0.00552727933973074, -0.024186939001083374, 0.004495158325880766, 0.007937825284898281, -0.012820028699934483, 0.028763581067323685, -0.02053377404808998, 0.009431684389710426, -0.003700696863234043, -0.03262045234441757, -0.002447892213240266, -0.001372483209706843, 0.009384152479469776, 0.004182805772870779, -0.0038840339984744787, 0.0054288203828036785, 0.03897614777088165, -0.027745040133595467, 0.026251181960105896, -0.02337210811674595, 0.0009107448859140277, -0.004977267235517502, 0.016962092369794846, 0.028899386525154114, -0.021579476073384285, 0.030773499980568886, 0.007611892186105251, -0.017885569483041763, -0.002108378568664193, 0.0038398972246795893, -0.0022441840264946222, 0.012887931428849697, -0.0007414125138893723, -0.007055089809000492, -0.030012991279363632, -0.02687588706612587, -0.01133975014090538,